# Highly Efficient and Air‐Stable Inorganic Perovskite Solar Cells Enabled by Polylactic Acid Modification

## abstract
Inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) suffer from serious carrier recombination and open‐circuit voltage loss because of surface defects and unfavorable energy level alignment. Herein, a polylactic acid (PLA) modification approach to improve the performance of mixed‐halide inorganic perovskites is reported. First, the surface defects are effectively passivated through strong interaction between C═O in PLA and undercoordinated Pb2+. Second, secondary grain growth is induced by PLA modification, resulting in larger grain sizes. Third, PLA modification makes the surface region of perovskite change from n‐ to p‐type, favoring charge transport from perovskite to the hole transport layer (HTL). The PLA modified films enable PSCs with less nonradiative recombination and lower energy loss. Consequently, record PCEs of 19.12% and 18.05% are achieved for CsPbI2.25Br0.75 and CsPbI2Br PSCs, respectively. The PSC with an active area of 1 cm2 shows a PCE of 16.41%. A PCE of 14.70% is achieved for HTL‐free PSC with carbon electrode. In addition, the PSC with PLA modification shows significantly improved air stability due to the hydrophobic PLA coating. This work suggests that PLA surface modification is an effective approach to achieving efficient, stable, scalable, and low‐cost inorganic PSCs.

Inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) suffer from serious carrier recombination and open-circuit voltage loss because of surface defects and unfavorable energy level alignment. Herein, a polylactic acid (PLA) modification approach to improve the performance of mixed-halide inorganic perovskites is reported. First, the surface defects are effectively passivated through strong interaction between $\scriptstyle\mathtt{c=o}$ in PLA and undercoordinated $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ . Second, secondary grain growth is induced by PLA modification, resulting in larger grain sizes. Third, PLA modification makes the surface region of perovskite change from n- to p-type, favoring charge transport from perovskite to the hole transport layer (HTL). The PLA modified films enable PSCs with less nonradiative recombination and lower energy loss. Consequently, record PCEs of $19.12\%$ and $18.05\%$ are achieved for $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{2.25}\mathsf{B r}_{0.75}$ and $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{2}\mathsf{B r}$ PSCs, respectively. The PSC with an active area of 1 $\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ shows a PCE of $16.47\%$ . A PCE of $14.70\%$ is achieved for HTL-free PSC with carbon electrode. In addition, the PSC with PLA modification shows significantly improved air stability due to the hydrophobic PLA coating. This work suggests that PLA surface modification is an effective approach to achieving efficient, stable, scalable, and low-cost inorganic PSCs.
## 1. Introduction
Over the last decade, organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) show tremendous enhancement in photovoltaic performance with a certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $26\%\cdot[1,2]$ However, volatile and unstable organic cations $\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3}^{+}}$ $(\mathrm{MA^{+}})$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{3}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}+$ $(\mathrm{FA^{+}}))$ make organic– inorganic hybrid PSCs vulnerable to high temperature and/or light illumination. Without instability issue caused by organic cations, inorganic perovskites $\mathsf{(C s P b X_{3}}$ , ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}}={\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}}$ , Br, and Cl) become very promising light absorption materials for PSCs. I and Br mixed inorganic perovskites $(\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ , $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.69}\mathrm{Br}_{0.31}$ , $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ etc.) $|^{[3-6]}$ show better phasestability than that of $\mathtt{B r}$ -poor inorganic perovskites $(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.85}\mathrm{Br}_{0.15}$ , etc.) and higher performance than that of Br-rich inorganic perovskites $(\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ ).[7–9] It is controversial whether I-rich inorganic perovskite is true inorganic material because of the possibility of organic–inorganic hybrid $\mathrm{Cs_{1-x}D M A_{x}P b I_{3}}$ formation if using DMAI or $\mathrm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{HPbI}_{3}}$ as raw materials.[10,11] Moreover, compared to $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ , mixed-halide inorganic perovskites are much easier to make and more suitable for indoor photovoltaic and tandem solar cells.[12–14]
In recent years, the reported efficient mixed-halide inorganic PSCs show PCEs of $\approx17\%$ for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs and $\approx18\%$ for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ PSCs.[15–17] In our previous work, a simple drop-coating method was developed to fabricate PSCs under ambient condition.[18–22] Additive-assisted airflow drying (AAD) was used to improve the PCEs for inorganic solar cells, yielding PCEs of $18.49\%$ and $17.39\%$ for $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ PSCs and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs, respectively.[23] Recently, the PCE of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs was enhanced to $17.8\%$ by using a novel hole transport layer (HTL) and post-cation exchange method.[24,25] These progresses suggest a good prospect for mixed-halide inorganic PSC. However, compared with organic–inorganic hybrid perovskites, mixed-halide inorganic perovskites are facing more challenges. First, mixed-halide inorganic perovskites have a wider bandgap, making it harder to find matched electron/hole transport materials, leading to large voltage loss. Second, the $\alpha$ -phase mixed-halide inorganic perovskites (especially I-rich perovskites) are unstable in humid air or even dry air (relative humidity $<20\%$ ) due to unfavored tolerance factor. Third, the square centimeterlevel mixed-halide inorganic perovskite solar cells are rarely reported.[26]
To address the above issues, lots of technologies were reported, such as introducing additives into perovskite solutions,[27–29] cation or anion doping,[30–32] interface engineering [6,15,24,25,33–37] (including HTL/perovskite interface, electron transport layer (ETL)/perovskite interface, and the surface of perovskite), etc. Among them, surface modification is one of the most significant for attaining efficient, stable, and scalable PSCs. Modification of hybrid perovskites by using chemicals with carbonyl $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{C=O})$ group (such as polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) and polylactic acid (PLA)) is a simple and effective strategy.[38,39] But the effectiveness of this strategy in inorganic perovskite solar cells has not been fully investigated yet.
Herein, we used PLAto modify the surface of inorganic perovskite films. The $\mathrm{C=O}$ ) groups in PLA can interact with undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ at the surface of perovskite and effectively passivate the surface defects (Figure 1a). The films with PLA modification show larger crystal grains because PLA may trigger a secondary crystallization during high-temperature annealing (Figure 1a). Moreover, PLA could adjust energy level alignment at the perovskite surface to facilitate carrier transporting (Figure 1b). After PLA modification, PCEs of $19.12\%$ and $18.05\%$ were achieved for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ PSCs and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs, which are both the record efficiencies for PSCs with the same perovskites. Thanks to the hydrophobicity of PLA, the PLA modified films and PSCs show better air stability. Moreover, the square centimeter-scale PSCs and HTL-free PSCs with carbon electrode also show remarkable PCEs, which indicates a very impressive prospect of this method for future commercialization.
## 2. Results and Discussion
Figure 1 shows a schematic of PLA surface modification process and its impacts on perovskite films. PLA was dissolved in chlorobenzene (CB) under continuous stirring at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 h. Then PLA solution was spin-coated onto perovskite films followed by annealing at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $1\mathrm{min}$ .
The influences of PLA surface modification on morphology of perovskite films were studied by Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM). The control film (without PLA surface modification) shows very small grains (Figure 2a,f). As shown in Figure 2b,g, when using $0.1\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}}$ PLA solution, the film does not show obvious change. As the concentration of PLA solution increases from 0.25 to $10\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , the grain sizes increase obviously and the grain boundaries become less but much clearer (Figure $2\mathrm{c}-$ e,h–j). The grain sizes enlarge to $1{-}2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ when concentration of PLA solution increases to $0.5~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ (Figure 2i and Figure S1, Supporting Information). We assume that the enlarged grain sizes and surface reconstruction are caused by secondary grain growth due to the high-temperature post-annealing with PLA. Clear surface reconstruction induced by secondary grain growth could be observed from SEM images of films prepared without lead acetate trihydrate additive (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The SEM image for the PLA-modified perovskite film before annealing was also measured (Figure S3a, Supporting Information). The morphology of the PLA-modified perovskite film before annealing is similar to the film without PLA. The grain size increases significantly after thermal annealing (Figure S3b, Supporting Information). Enlarged grains can also be observed in the AFM images (Figure S3e, Supporting Information). However, as highlighted by red circle in Figure 2c,d, PLA would aggregate on the perovskite surface if the concentration of PLA solution is too high. The surface is very rough with large grain boundaries when concentration of PLA solution increases to $10~\mathrm{{mg~mL^{-1}}}$ (Figure 2e,j). The thick PLA layer may influence carrier transporting and light absorption and thus cause decrease of PCE for PSCs (see performance study of PSCs later in this article). Therefore, the concentration of PLA has to be carefully controlled. In the cross-sectional images of the films (Figure 2k–o), all the films are dense with no voids at the substrate/perovskite interface. The films treated by using $0.25{-}10\mathrm{mg\:mL^{-1}}$ PLA solution show less grain boundaries, which is consistent with the top-view images. The highly vertically oriented grains could promote carrier transport and suppress nonradiative recombination.
The $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) measurement was performed to study the influences of PLA surface modification on the crystal structure of perovskite films. The control films and the films with PLA surface modification all show two main diffraction peaks at $\approx14.7^{\circ}$ and ${\approx}29.5^{\circ}$ (Figure 3a, inset plot), assigning to (100) and (200) planes of $\alpha$ -phase perovskite, respectively. The positions of XRD peaks for films with low concentration PLA solution $_{(0.1-}$ $0.5\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ ) are the same, indicating that PLA did not incorporate into the perovskite structure. The full width at half maximum (FWHM) of XRD main peaks (Table S1, Supporting Information) decreases for the films treated by using $0.25~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ PLA solution, indicating improved crystallinity and enlarged crystallite size. When using $10\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}}$ PLA solution, the XRD peaks of films shift to a low degree and show much larger FWHM, which may be because that excessive PLA invades into perovskite and reduces the crystallinity of perovskite. The PLA-modified film shows a much lower (110) peak after thermal annealing, suggesting improved preferred orientation (Figure S4a, Supporting Information). A possible mechanism is proposed by combining the XRD and the SEM data. The PLA forms a complex with perovskite at the grain surface. Secondary crystallization happens after thermal annealing, leading to enlarged grains (Figure S4b, Supporting Information). As shown in UV–vis absorption spectra (Figure 3b), when treated by using PLA solutions with moderate concentration, the films show enhanced absorption because of the enlarged grain size, higher crystallinity, and denser films.
Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra were measured to study the effects of PLA modification on nonradiative recombination of perovskite films. The films with PLA modification prepared on bare glass substrate produce higher PL intensity than the control film, indicating suppressed nonradiative recombination (Figure 3c). According to SEM, XRD, UV–vis, and PL results, the films treated by using $0.25~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ PLA solution show enlarged grains, the highest crystallinity, improved light absorption, and the least nonradiative recombination, making them the optimal choice for further study and fabricating PSCs. Therefore, perovskite films modified by $0.25\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ PLA were selected for further study (the films were noted as “modified” hereinafter). Carrier lifetime was measured by using time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL). As shown in Figure 3d, the average PL lifetime is prolonged from 18.9 to 22.1 ns, with prolonged $\tau_{2}$ (from 19.4 to $22.5~\mathrm{ns}^{\prime}$ ) and enhanced proportion of $\tau_{2}$ (from $37.2\%$ to $53.1\%\rvert$ , indicating decreased trap density in the modified film, which benefits charge transport and performance of PSCs.
The interaction between PLA and perovskite films was studied by the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As shown in Figure 4a of C 1s spectra, the control film shows a single peak at $282{\mathrm{~eV}}$ while the modified film displays three peaks located between 252 and $288~\mathrm{eV_{\it\mathrm{i}}}$ which are similar to the film treated by using $10\mathrm{{mg}\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ PLA solution. Combined with the wide peaks located at $532\ \mathrm{eV}$ in O 1s spectra of the modified films and the film treated by using $10\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ PLA solution (Figure 4b), the presence of PLA in the modified film can be concluded. The $\mathrm{{Pb}}$ 4f peaks of the modified films shift towards lower binding energy (Figure 4c), which suggests the strong interaction between $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ in PLA and under-coordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , revealing that trap states caused by under-coordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ at the perovskite surface can be efficiently passivated. The peaks in I 3d, Br 3d, and Cs 3d spectra show no shift after PLA modification (Figure S5, Supporting Information), which suggests that PLA only interacts with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) was used to confirm the exiting of PLA in the modified films. As shown in Figure 4d, the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ (around $1730\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and C─O (1100–1300 $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ) stretching vibration of PLA could be observed in the modified films and the film treated by using $10\mathrm{{mg}\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ PLA solution, suggesting that PLA remains in the modified film.
The impacts of PLA surface modification on energy band were revealed by ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurement (Figure $_{4\mathrm{e-g}}$ . The valence band maximum (VB) of perovskite film moves from $-5.27\ \mathrm{eV}$ to $-5.17\ \mathrm{eV}$ after PLA modification, which is closer to the highest occupied molecular orbital of PTAA $(-5.1\ \mathrm{eV})$ . The better energy level alignment between perovskite and HTL is beneficial for hole transport. Moreover, the $E_{\mathrm{{F}}}$ shifts from close to conduction band minimum (CB) to near VB after PLA modification, which suggests that the perovskite surface region changes from strong n-type to weak p-type. The work function measured by using Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) increases from 4.8 to $5.2\mathrm{eV}$ after PLA modification (Figure S6, Supporting Information), which is consistent with the UPS results. Holes are much easier to transfer from a weak p-type semiconductor (the modified film) to a strong p-type semiconductor (PTAA) than from a strong n-type semiconductor (the control film) to a strong p-type semiconductor. With better morphology, less defects, and better energy level alignment, the modified films possess good prospect for constructing efficient PSCs.
We fabricated inorganic PSC with the device structure of $\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}/Z n O/}$ perovskite/PTAA/ $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}/\mathrm{Ag}$ (Figure 5a). PSCs based on the films treated by using $0.25~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ PLA solution and annealing at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $1\mathrm{min}$ display the best PCE (Figures S7–S9, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 5b, PSC based on the control $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ film displays a PCE of $17.81\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ , short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ , and FF of $1.308~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ $16.70\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and $81.53\%$ , respectively. The champion PSC based on the modified $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ films shows a PCE of $19.12\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ , $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ , and FF of $1.371\mathrm{~V},17.12\mathrm{~mA~cm}^{-2}$ , and $81.45\%$ . We also prepared PSCs based on the control and modified $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ films, achieving PCEs of $16.98\%$ and $18.06\%$ , respectively (Figure 5c). The main cause for the better PCEs of PSCs based on the modified films is attributed to the enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . The average $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ increases from 1.29 to $1.37\mathrm{V}$ for $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ PSCs and from 1.31 to $1.38{\mathrm{V}}{}$ for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs (inset plots in Figure 5b,c), which is mainly caused by better film morphology, fewer surface defects, improved carriers transporting, and better energy level alignment as we demonstrated above. Performance of the reported high-efficiency $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs is summarized in Table 1 and Figure 5d, The PCEs for $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs in this work exceed the reported results. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of the champion cells are shown in Figure 5e. The integrated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ calculated from the EQE spectra are 16.35 and $15.22\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs, respectively. The integrated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ obtained from the EQE measurement matches well with the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ obtained from the $J{-}V$ curves (Figure S13, Supporting Information). Steady-state PCEs of $18.7\%$ and $17.6\%$ were achieved for the champion $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs (Figure S12, Supporting Information). Moreover, large-area (1 $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ PSC shows a champion PCE of $16.41\%$ (Figure 5f), which is among the highest PCE for wide-bandgap inorganic PSCs with an active area of $1\mathrm{cm}^{2.[26]}$
To deeply understand the reasons for the enhanced $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ electron-only devices $(\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /perovskite/ $/\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}/\mathrm{Ag})$ and hole-only devices (ITO/PEDOT:PSS/perovskite/ PTAA/Ag) were fabricated to estimate the trap densities in the devices by using the space-charge-limited current (SCLC) method. The electron-only device based on the modified film shows a much lower trap-filled limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ of $0.152\mathrm{~V~}$ than that of the control device (0.944 V; Figure 6a), with trap density decreasing from $9.26\times10^{15}$ (control device) to $1.49\times10^{15}~\mathrm{{cm}^{-3}}$ (modified device). The hole-only device shows the same tendency ( $\cdot V_{\mathrm{{TFL}}}$ of $0.367~\mathrm{V}$ and $0.546\mathrm{~V~}$ for the control and modified devices) (Figure 6b), with trap density decreasing from $5.36\times10^{15}$ (control device) to $3.60\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ (modified device). The carrier recombination in PSCs was studied by measuring the light intensity dependence of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . The value of ideal factor $(n,V_{\mathrm{oc}}=n\mathrm{kTln(I)/q}+c$ onstant) is related to trap-assisted recombination while the value of $\alpha$ $U_{\mathrm{sc}}\propto^{\alpha})$ is a factor related to bimolecular recombination. The PSC based on the control film displays $n$ of 1.323 and $\alpha$ of 0.985 while the PSC based on the modified film shows $n$ of 1.248 and $\alpha$ of 0.997 (Figure 6c,d). The lower ideal factor indicates reduced trap-assisted recombination in the PSC based on the modified film. The $\alpha$ value for PSC based on the modified film is closer to unity, suggesting less radiative recombination in devices. These results imply that the reduced trap density and recombination also contribute to the enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and PCE.
Due to the low tolerance factor $(\approx0.82)$ , the mixed-halide inorganic perovskite (especially I-rich perovskites) is unstable when directly exposed to moisture or even dry air (relative humidity $<20\%)$ . The effect of PLA modification on moisture stability of PSCs was studied. PSCs were stored in air $(\mathrm{RH}=15\mathrm{-}20\%)$ without encapsulation. As shown in Figure $\mathrm{7a}$ , the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ PSC based on the modified film retains $92\%$ of its initial PCE after being stored in air for 62 days while the PSC based on the control film loses $60\%$ of the initial PCE after 60 days. The enhanced air stability may be caused by the hydrophobicity of PLA layer on perovskite surface, which was confirmed by the larger contact angle for the modified films (Figure S14, Supporting Information). The reasons for PCE loss during storage in air are also revealed. For the control film stored in air, the light absorption starts decreasing at the first ten days and remains less than $50\%$ of the initial absorbance after 60 days (Figure 7b). The modified film only shows slight light absorption reduction after 60 days (Figure 7c). The potential phase separation of iodide and bromide for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ film was characterized through PL spectral under slow scan speed. Both fresh control and modified films exhibit a main peak at $670~\mathrm{nm}$ and a shoulder peak at $657~\mathrm{nm}$ , which may be caused by phase separation (Figure 7d). For the control film, the intensity of the shoulder peak increases to the same with that of the main peak after 30 days, indicating the expansion of phase separation (Figure 7e). Finally, the peak located at $657~\mathrm{nm}$ becomes main peak after being stored in air for 60 days. For the modified film, the main peak is still dominated after being stored in air for 60 days (Figure 7f), indicating the phase separation caused by humidity is hindered due to the hydrophobicity of PLA coating. At last, the crystal structures of the films stored in air for 60 days were characterized by XRD. The FWHM of main XRD peak of the control film is much larger than that of the modified film, indicating that crystallinity of perovskite is enhanced by PLA. Therefore, we conclude that hydrophobic PLA is helpful for enhancing air stability of inorganic perovskite via suppressing phase separation or phase transition.
Carbon-based HTL-free inorganic PSC with a structure of $\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}/Z n O/C s P b I_{2.25}B r_{0.75}/}$ Carbon was also made (Figure 8a). Carbon electrode with a thickness of ${\approx}35~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ was painted onto perovskite films (Figure S15, Supporting Information). The best device gives a good PCE of $14.70\%$ (Figure 8b), which is among the highest for carbon-based HTL-free $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ cells. An integrated current density of $14.72\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ was obtained from the EQE spectra, which is consistent with the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ from $J{-}V$ curves (Figure 8c). The PCE of the carbon-based cells is lower than that of the cells with metal electrode. The absence of expensive noble metals and HTM, as well as vacuum-free fabrication process can reduce the cost significantly.[46] The performance of carbon electrode HTL-free PSC could be further enhanced through modifying carbon electrode, including adjusting thickness, optimizing contact with perovskite films, improving conductivity, etc.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we developed a PLA modification strategy to improve the performance of mixed halide inorganic PSCs. PLA could passivate the surface defect and induce secondary grain growth during high-temperature annealing. The films with PLA surface modification have larger grains, higher crystallinity, and less defects than the control film. The fermi level of perovskite could be adjusted by PLA modification, which facilitate the carrier transporting between HTL and perovskite films. Based on PLA surface modification, remarkable PCEs of $19.12\%$ and $18.05\%$ were achieved for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs, respectively. To the best of our knowledge, the PCEs are the highest for PSCs with the same perovskite layer. Moreover, the PSC with an active area of $\mathrm{1cm^{2}}$ shows a PCE of $16.41\%$ . In addition, a PCE of $14.70\%$ was achieved for HTL-free PSC with carbon electrode. The air stability of PSCs is significantly enhanced due to the hydrophobically PLA coating. Our work paves a way for developing highly-efficient, air-stable, scalable, and low-cost mixed-halide inorganic PSCs.
